      "problem": "Solve for $C$ given that $y(t) = C \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t}$ and $y(5)=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We substitute $t=5$ and $y(t)=1$ into the expression for $y(t),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =C\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{5}}{{2}^{5}} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot \\frac{1}{32} \\\\ 1\\cdot 32 & =C \\\\ 32 & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=32.$",
